# Overview
#
# `has to` and `have to` state an obligation without saying how strong
# it is, so the reader cannot tell a requirement from advice. The fix
# writes `must` for a requirement and `should` for advice, the grades
# the technical-writing skill defines, or a bare command for a step.
# When the subject is the system, the fix states what it does in the
# present tense (`the runtime has to pull the image` becomes `the
# runtime pulls the image`). The rule sits at Optional, because the
# phrases often state a reason or a burden that `must` does not make
# clearer (`because we have to wait for the whole response`).
#
# Expression
#
# `has to` and `have to` carry a guard on the word before them, and
# `hneg` lists the words that the guard skips. Weir has no lookbehind,
# so the filter consumes the word before `has` or `have` and narrows the
# finding back to the phrase. A second guard skips `do` and `offer`
# after the phrase.
#
# False Positives
#
# The guard before the phrase skips a word that states that no
# obligation exists, such as a negation (`you do not have to restart`,
# `you won't have to restart`) or `necessarily`. It skips `only` and
# `just`, which state what suffices (`you only have to set the flag
# once`, `the application just has to reload the token`). It skips
# `rarely`, which states that a step is rare (`you rarely have to
# annotate types`), and `to`, which names a burden (`we don't want to
# have to update the test`). In the measure.py corpus, `just` sits
# before the phrase on 1 line, which states what suffices. `rarely` does
# not sit before the phrase on any line.
#
# A modal verb is a helping verb that marks possibility, ability, or
# obligation, such as `can`, `might`, or `must`. The guard skips a modal
# verb that marks a possibility, such as `might`, `could`, or `would`
# and its contraction (`you'd have to`). The sentence then describes a
# case that might not arise.
#
# `has to do with` and `has to offer` pass, because they state no
# obligation.
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Recommended ProseObligation reports `need to` and the other
# phrases that read as advice. The Optional ProseObligation reports `has
# to` and `have to`, which often state a reason or a burden instead.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 147 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 15 of 20 sampled
# hits stated obligations that `must` or the present tense states more
# clearly. Of the five misfires, one stated a reason (`because you have
# to remember two rules`), and one stated what suffices (`we have to
# look in only one place`). One denied an obligation (`does not even
# have to be running`), one used `have` for owning (`any automation you
# have to use`), and one was a figure of speech (`I have to ask`).
expr hneg [not, never, don't, doesn't, didn't, shouldn't, won't, wouldn't, would, we'd, you'd, necessarily, might, may, could, to, only, just, rarely]
expr main <(!@hneg [has, have] to ![do, offer]), ([has, have] to)>
let message "Vague obligation. Write `must` or `should` for what a person does, the present tense for what the system does, or open with `To` and the goal."
let description "Flags `has to` and `have to`, which hide how strong an obligation is."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The fix has to do with the cache."
allows "You would have to restart the node."
allows "You won't have to restart the node."
allows "See what the release has to offer."
allows "PVCs do not necessarily have to request a class."
allows "You only have to set the flag once."
allows "The application just has to reload the token."
allows "You rarely have to annotate types."
